//? Title: Meta-regression
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Explain between-study heterogeneity with study-level moderators, separate explained from residual heterogeneity, find the studies that drive the fit, and avoid the false discoveries that few studies make easy

Meta-regression extends the random-effects model by letting the average effect depend on study characteristics \(x_i\):

\[y_i\sim N(x_i'\beta,\;v_i+\tau^2).\]

Here \(\tau^2\) is now the *residual* heterogeneity, the part the moderators do not explain. The coefficients are weighted least squares with weights \(1/(v_i+\hat\tau^2)\). Three summaries describe the fit:

- the omnibus test \(Q_M\) of all moderator coefficients;
- the test \(Q_E\) for residual heterogeneity;
- the pseudo-\(R^2\), the proportional reduction in \(\tau^2\) from the intercept-only model.

Influence diagnostics refit the model without each study. They include externally studentised residuals, DFFITS, Cook's distance, covariance ratios, DFBETAS, and the change in \(\tau^2\) and \(Q_E\).

`meta_regression` takes a moderator matrix and adds the intercept itself. It supports every \(\tau^2\) estimator and Wald or Hartung–Knapp inference, and it can return `metafor`-style influence diagnostics, including metafor's influence flag. Moderators are centred and scaled internally. This matters in practice: with publication year as a moderator, the uncentred normal equations lose about seven digits, so the internal fit uses standardised moderators and maps the results back to the original scale. The tests reproduce `metafor::rma(mods = ...)` with REML and with DL plus Hartung–Knapp, along with `confint()`, `rstandard()`, `fitted()` and every column of `influence()`, including an added outlier that metafor flags.

The example regresses the BCG log risk ratios on absolute latitude, the classic explanation of the trials' heterogeneity. The simulation uses an irrelevant moderator \(x\sim U(0,1)\) (\(\beta_1=0\)) or a relevant one (\(\beta_1=0.5\)), with \(v_i\sim U(0.01,0.2)\) and residual \(\tau^2\in\{0.04,0.16\}\), and 4000 meta-analyses per setting. It records the rejection rate of the 5% slope test under Wald and HK inference, and the pseudo-\(R^2\) obtained when the moderator is irrelevant.

```{cpp, id=mr, file="figures/meta_regression.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/meta_analysis.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::MetaInference;

int main() {
    // BCG trials: cases and non-cases (vaccinated, control) and absolute latitude.
    const double bcg[13][5] = {{4, 119, 11, 128, 44},        {6, 300, 29, 274, 55},
                               {3, 228, 11, 209, 42},        {62, 13536, 248, 12619, 52},
                               {33, 5036, 47, 5761, 13},     {180, 1361, 372, 1079, 44},
                               {8, 2537, 10, 619, 19},       {505, 87886, 499, 87892, 13},
                               {29, 7470, 45, 7232, 27},     {17, 1699, 65, 1600, 42},
                               {186, 50448, 141, 27197, 18}, {5, 2493, 3, 2338, 33},
                               {27, 16886, 29, 17825, 33}};
    std::vector<double> y, v, lat;
    dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> x(13, 1);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 13; ++i) {
        const auto& t = bcg[i];
        const double n1 = t[0] + t[1], n0 = t[2] + t[3];
        y.push_back(std::log((t[0] / n1) / (t[2] / n0)));
        v.push_back(1 / t[0] - 1 / n1 + 1 / t[2] - 1 / n0);
        lat.push_back(t[4]);
        x(i, 0) = t[4];
    }
    const auto r = dm::stats::meta_regression(
        y, v, x, {.model = {.inference = MetaInference::hartung_knapp}, .influence = true});
    std::println("Log risk ratio = {:.3f} {:+.4f} x latitude (SE {:.4f}, t = {:.2f} on {} "
                 "df, p = {:.4f}). Residual tau2 = {:.4f} [{:.4f}, {:.4f}], residual "
                 "I2 = {:.1f}%, QE = {:.1f} (p = {:.3f}), pseudo-R2 = {:.1f}%.\n",
                 r.coefficients[0], r.coefficients[1], r.standard_errors[1],
                 r.statistics[1], r.df, r.p_values[1], r.tau2, r.tau2_lower, r.tau2_upper,
                 r.i2, r.qe, r.qe_p_value, r.r2);
    std::vector<std::size_t> order(13);
    std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
    std::ranges::sort(order, [&](auto a, auto b) {
        return r.influence.cooks_distance[a] > r.influence.cooks_distance[b];
    });
    std::println("| Study (latitude) | Weight (%) | Hat | Studentized residual | DFFITS | "
                 "Cook's D | tau2 without it | Flagged |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |");
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
        const auto i = order[j];
        const auto& inf = r.influence;
        std::println("| {} ({}°) | {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | "
                     "{} |",
                     i + 1, lat[i], inf.weight[i], inf.hat[i], inf.studentized_residuals[i],
                     inf.dffits[i], inf.cooks_distance[i], inf.tau2_deleted[i],
                     inf.influential[i] ? "yes" : "no");
    }

    std::println("\n| tau2 | k | Size: Wald | Size: HK | Power (slope 0.5): Wald | HK | "
                 "Mean pseudo-R2, irrelevant moderator (%) | Pseudo-R2 > 25% |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(12);
    for (const double tau2 : {0.04, 0.16})
        for (const std::size_t k : {6, 10, 20, 40}) {
            double sw = 0, sh = 0, pw = 0, ph = 0, r2 = 0, r2big = 0;
            const int reps = 4000;
            for (int rep = 0; rep < reps; ++rep)
                for (const double slope : {0.0, 0.5}) {
                    std::vector<double> yy(k), vv(k);
                    dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> xx(k, 1);
                    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
                        xx(i, 0) = rng.uniform01();
                        vv[i] = 0.01 + 0.19 * rng.uniform01();
                        yy[i] = 0.2 + slope * xx(i, 0) + std::sqrt(tau2) * rng.normal() +
                                std::sqrt(vv[i]) * rng.normal();
                    }
                    const auto w = dm::stats::meta_regression(yy, vv, xx);
                    const auto h = dm::stats::meta_regression(
                        yy, vv, xx, {.model = {.inference = MetaInference::hartung_knapp}});
                    if (slope == 0) {
                        sw += w.p_values[1] <= 0.05, sh += h.p_values[1] <= 0.05;
                        r2 += w.r2 / reps, r2big += w.r2 > 25;
                    } else {
                        pw += w.p_values[1] <= 0.05, ph += h.p_values[1] <= 0.05;
                    }
                }
            std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.1f} | "
                         "{:.3f} |", tau2, k, sw / reps, sh / reps, pw / reps, ph / reps,
                         r2, r2big / reps);
        }

    // Bubble plot with the fitted line, its 95% band and the 95% prediction band.
    std::vector<double> gx, fit, lo, hi, plo, phi;
    const double t = 2.200985160082949;  // t(0.975, 11)
    for (double g = 10; g <= 57; g += 0.5) {
        const double f = r.coefficients[0] + r.coefficients[1] * g;
        const double var = r.covariance(0, 0) + 2 * g * r.covariance(0, 1) +
                           g * g * r.covariance(1, 1);
        gx.push_back(g), fit.push_back(f);
        lo.push_back(f - t * std::sqrt(var)), hi.push_back(f + t * std::sqrt(var));
        plo.push_back(f - t * std::sqrt(var + r.tau2));
        phi.push_back(f + t * std::sqrt(var + r.tau2));
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.ribbon(gx, plo, phi, {176, 72, 43, 0.12})
        .ribbon(gx, lo, hi, {26, 90, 147, 0.3})
        .line(gx, fit, "Fitted log risk ratio (95% CI and prediction bands)",
              {26, 90, 147}, 2);
    const double wmax = *std::ranges::max_element(r.weights);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 13; ++i)
        plot.points({lat[i]}, {y[i]}, "", {60, 60, 60, 0.75},
                    4 + 14 * std::sqrt(r.weights[i] / wmax));
    plot.abline_h(0.0, {150, 150, 150})
        .title("BCG trials: effect against absolute latitude")
        .x_label("Absolute latitude (degrees); point area proportional to weight")
        .y_label("Log risk ratio")
        .size(680, 420);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/meta_regression.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Log risk ratio = 0.251 -0.0291 x latitude (SE 0.0082, t = -3.55 on 11 df, p = 0.0046). Residual tau2 = 0.0763 [0.0167, 0.7848], residual I2 = 68.4%, QE = 30.7 (p = 0.001), pseudo-R2 = 75.6%.

