| 6 (44°) | 13.6 | 0.237 | 0.93 | 0.54 | 0.222 | 0.0760 | no |
| 8 (13°) | 14.1 | 0.357 | 0.54 | 0.44 | 0.209 | 0.1068 | no |
| 4 (52°) | 11.8 | 0.348 | -0.63 | -0.47 | 0.192 | 0.0897 | no |

| tau2 | k | Size: Wald | Size: HK | Power (slope 0.5): Wald | HK | Mean pseudo-R2, irrelevant moderator (%) | Pseudo-R2 > 25% |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.04 | 6 | 0.083 | 0.065 | 0.187 | 0.128 | 18.0 | 0.218 |
| 0.04 | 10 | 0.076 | 0.060 | 0.258 | 0.203 | 14.0 | 0.184 |
| 0.04 | 20 | 0.068 | 0.058 | 0.441 | 0.402 | 10.1 | 0.137 |
| 0.04 | 40 | 0.068 | 0.061 | 0.727 | 0.710 | 6.6 | 0.084 |
| 0.16 | 6 | 0.112 | 0.063 | 0.171 | 0.092 | 17.4 | 0.234 |
| 0.16 | 10 | 0.090 | 0.059 | 0.192 | 0.137 | 11.1 | 0.162 |
| 0.16 | 20 | 0.075 | 0.059 | 0.262 | 0.225 | 5.2 | 0.067 |
| 0.16 | 40 | 0.059 | 0.050 | 0.441 | 0.416 | 2.2 | 0.009 |
\image(figures/meta_regression.svg)
// result_end

Latitude explains most of the BCG heterogeneity. The log risk ratio falls by 0.029 per degree from the equator, so a trial at 50° has a risk ratio about 0.35 times that of a trial at 15° (\(e^{-0.029\times35}\)). The HK test gives \(t=-3.55\) on 11 df (\(p=0.005\)). Residual \(\tau^2\) drops from 0.31 to 0.076, a pseudo-\(R^2\) of 76%. The fit leaves meaningful heterogeneity (\(Q_E=30.7\), \(p=0.001\), residual \(I^2=68\%\)), and the prediction band still spans a factor of about three in risk ratio at any latitude. No study meets metafor's influence criteria. The largest Cook's distance belongs to the Vandiviere trial (19°), whose effect is much stronger than its latitude predicts (studentised residual \(-2.67\)). Without it, residual \(\tau^2\) halves to 0.041. The two most precise trials (TPT Madras at 13° and Hart & Sutherland at 52°) have the highest leverage, because they sit at the ends of the latitude range.

The simulation adds a warning about moderator analyses with few studies:

- **Wald tests are anti-conservative.** The 5% slope test rejects 7–11% of the time with up to 10 studies. HK brings this to 6–6.5%, at a modest cost in power that shrinks as \(k\) grows.
- **Power is low.** Even a moderator that shifts the effect by 0.5 across its range is detected only 13–26% of the time with 10 or fewer studies.
- **Pseudo-\(R^2\) is unreliable.** With an irrelevant moderator, the pseudo-\(R^2\) averages 14–18% for 6–10 studies, and more than 18% of such meta-analyses report over 25% of heterogeneity "explained". \(\hat\tau^2\) is so variable with few studies that removing one degree of freedom often lowers it by chance.

>> Practical guidance

- Pre-specify a small number of moderators with a scientific rationale. With fewer than about 10 studies per moderator, treat meta-regression as exploratory.
- Use Hartung–Knapp inference (with \(t_{k-p}\) and an \(F\) omnibus test) for meta-regression with few studies, and report residual \(\tau^2\) with its interval as well as the \(Q_E\) test.
- Do not interpret pseudo-\(R^2\) with few studies. An irrelevant moderator routinely appears to explain a quarter of the heterogeneity.
- Moderator associations are observational across studies, even when each study is randomised. They are vulnerable to ecological confounding: latitude is correlated with trial era, allocation method and exposure to environmental mycobacteria.
- Inspect influence diagnostics, especially studentised residuals, Cook's distance and \(\tau^2\) after deletion. Report a sensitivity analysis when one study changes the residual heterogeneity or the slope materially.

>> References

- S. G. Thompson and J. P. T. Higgins (2002). How should meta-regression analyses be undertaken and interpreted? *Statistics in Medicine* 21(11), 1559-1573. doi:10.1002/sim.1187
- W. Viechtbauer and M. W.-L. Cheung (2010). Outlier and influence diagnostics for meta-analysis. *Research Synthesis Methods* 1(2), 112-125. doi:10.1002/jrsm.11
- G. Knapp and J. Hartung (2003). Improved tests for a random effects meta-regression with a single covariate. *Statistics in Medicine* 22(17), 2693-2710. doi:10.1002/sim.1482
- J. P. T. Higgins and S. G. Thompson (2004). Controlling the risk of spurious findings from meta-regression. *Statistics in Medicine* 23(11), 1663-1682. doi:10.1002/sim.1752
- G. A. Colditz, T. F. Brewer, C. S. Berkey, et al. (1994). Efficacy of BCG vaccine in the prevention of tuberculosis: meta-analysis of the published literature. *JAMA* 271(9), 698-702. doi:10.1001/jama.1994.03510330076038
